## Proof

By the Artin–Wedderburn theorem, every central simple $k$-algebra $A$ is isomorphic to $M_n(D)$ for some central division $k$-algebra $D$ and some positive integer $n$. In particular $A$ is a simple Artinian ring.

**Structure of $A$-modules.** A simple Artinian ring has, up to isomorphism, a unique simple left module. Concretely, for $A = M_n(D)$ this unique simple left module is the natural module $V = D^n$ of column vectors, on which $A$ acts by left multiplication. Since $A$ is semisimple, every left $A$-module is semisimple, hence a direct sum of copies of $V$. Therefore every left $A$-module is isomorphic to $V^{\oplus m}$ for a uniquely determined integer $m \geq 0$ (the multiplicity is unique because $V$ is the only simple module, so the isomorphism type is determined by $m$).

**Dimension determines $m$.** The $k$-dimension of the simple module is
$$\dim_k V = n \cdot \dim_k D.$$
Hence
$$\dim_k(V^{\oplus m}) = m \cdot n \cdot \dim_k D.$$
Since $n \geq 1$ and $\dim_k D \geq 1$, the factor $n \cdot \dim_k D$ is a positive integer. Thus if two $A$-modules $M_1 \cong V^{\oplus m_1}$ and $M_2 \cong V^{\oplus m_2}$ satisfy $\dim_k M_1 = \dim_k M_2$, then
$$m_1 \cdot n \cdot \dim_k D = m_2 \cdot n \cdot \dim_k D \implies m_1 = m_2,$$
so $M_1 \cong M_2$ as $A$-modules.

**Conclusion.** Any two $A$-modules of the same dimension over $k$ are isomorphic as $A$-modules. $\blacksquare$

